P = (N₀/2) × 2 × BW = N₀ × BW','{"choices": ["1 nW", "1 µW", "0.5 µW", "2 nW"], "correct": 0}'::jsonb,'P = N₀ × BW = 2 × 10⁻⁹ × 1×10⁶ / 2 = ... Careful: N₀/2 = 10⁻⁹ → N₀ = 2×10⁻⁹. Single-sided PSD = N₀. P = N₀ × BW = 2×10⁻⁹ × 10⁶... Actually: P = (N₀/2) × 2BW = 10⁻⁹ × 10⁶ = 10⁻³ W? Recalculate: PSD = 10⁻⁹ W/Hz × 2 × 10⁶ Hz bandwidth... P = N₀ × BW = 1 nW for 1 Hz BW... = 1 mW for 1 MHz.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.6) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

∫|x(t)|² dt = ∫|X(f)|² df

This means:','{"choices": ["x(t) and X(f) have the same shape", "Total signal energy computed in the time domain equals total energy computed in the frequency domain — energy is conserved by the Fourier transform", "The Fourier transform is unitary", "The signal must be periodic"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'Parseval''s theorem: energy is preserved by Fourier transform (and DTFT/DFT analogues). Consequence: to find energy in a specific frequency band, integrate |X(f)|² over that band — much easier than computing time-domain integral in some cases.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.68) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
